 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Near-real-time sampling at test time due to the feedforward nature of the model [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the diffusion model and VAE decoder; latent space compression reduces memory compared to raw Gaussian fields [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Amortizes reconstruction cost; promotes multi-view coherence; enables direct text/image-to-3D generation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to imperfect cross-view consistency (noisy geometry in occluded regions); requires large curated 3D datasets [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When real-time generation from text/image is required and multi-view coherence is critical.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high-fidelity geometry in complex occluded regions is required without post-processing, or when training data is scarce [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Near-real-time sampling at test time, amortizing the reconstruction cost [S1]. Training requires large-scale data and computational resources [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians and the capacity of the feedforward/diffusion model [S1, S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Promotes multi-view coherence, handles occlusions better than iterative methods, allows direct manipulation of explicit primitives [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires large curated 3D datasets, struggles with controllability (disentangling geometry/appearance), and faces challenges in the high-dimensional output space [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When rapid generation from text/image is needed, or when multi-view coherence is critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction from a specific set of multi-view images is required without pre-trained priors.
 
 ## Background
-Direct 3D generation methods aim to learn a feedforward or diffusion model that outputs Gaussian fields directly from text or images, without an explicit intermediate image set [S1]. This approach addresses the limitations of two-stage pipelines by optimizing directly for the Gaussian output space [S1]. The field has evolved to include dynamic 4D generation (e.g., AYG) and latent space diffusion models (e.g., DiffGS) to handle high-dimensional structured outputs [S2, S3].
+Direct 3D Generation emerged as a response to the computational cost and limitations of iterative 3DGS optimization. It builds on the success of diffusion models in 2D image generation and extends them to 3D explicit representations [S1, S3]. Key developments include the use of Gaussian VAEs for latent space compression [S3] and the integration of text-to-image/video models for dynamic content [S2].
 
